  "plan": "First, look at the hexdump file contents. Then reconstruct the binary from it, identify its type, and execute it. Let me start by viewing the file.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cd /app && ls -la && head -50 data.hex\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The hexdump is in the default `hexdump` format: octal 16-bit words, 8 per line, with octal offsets (note offsets increment by 020=16 decimal). The very first word 000407 (octal) = 0o0407 = the classic a.out OMAGIC magic number, indicating a VAX/PDP-11 a.out executable. I need to reconstruct the binary from the octal words, then identify and run it.",
  "plan": "Write a Python script to reconstruct the binary. Each octal word represents a 16-bit little-endian value (the byte order hexdump used when producing the dump). I'll reconstruct, then validate by re-running `hexdump` on the result and diffing against data.hex. If it matches, the byte order is correct. Then I'll run `file` to confirm the type and check what emulators are available to run it.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > /app/reconstruct.py << 'EOF'
out = bytearray()
with open('/app/data.hex') as f:
    for line in f:
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        parts = line.split()
        for p in parts[1:]:
            val = int(p, 8)
            out += val.to_bytes(2, 'little')
with open('/app/data.bin', 'wb') as g:
    g.write(out)
print('wrote', len(out), 'bytes')
EOF
python3 /app/reconstruct.py
",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "hexdump /app/data.bin > /app/data.recheck && diff /app/data.hex /app/data.recheck && echo MATCH
",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "file /app/data.bin
",
      "duration": 0.3
    }
  ]
}

